.TH NESTWG 1 "August 2026" "nestwg" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nestwg \- create and manage nested WireGuard VPN chains
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nestwg connect
.RB [ --wait " duration]
.IR chain.yaml
.br
.B nestwg validate
.IR chain.yaml
.br
.B nestwg plan
.IR chain.yaml
.br
.B nestwg up
.RB [ --wait " duration]
.IR chain.yaml
.br
.B nestwg attach
.I chain-name
.B --route
.I CIDR
.RB [ --route " CIDR...]
.br
.B nestwg detach
.IR chain-name
.br
.B nestwg down
.IR chain-name
.br
.B nestwg status
.RI [ chain-name ]
.br
.B nestwg diagnose
.IR chain-name
.br
.B nestwg exec
.I chain-name
.B --
.I command
.RI [ arguments... ]
.br
.B nestwg shell
.IR chain-name
.br
.B nestwg doctor
.br
.B nestwg recover
.I chain-name
.br
.B nestwg version
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nestwg
connects a Linux terminal or selected commands through ordered WireGuard VPN
hops. Each inner VPN connection is forced through the previous hop. The normal
host network is not changed, and selected VPN traffic cannot fall back to it if
a hop fails.
.PP
The chain document orders hops from the outermost entry to the innermost exit.
Endpoint hostnames are resolved and pinned before privileged network changes.
Plans and runtime state do not contain private or preshared keys.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B connect
Configure every VPN hop, initiate the nested handshakes, wait up to ten seconds
by default, and open the invoking user's shell through the VPN. Set
.B --wait 0
to open the shell immediately. Exiting the shell disconnects and removes the
VPN automatically. The shell receives NESTWG_VPN=1 and NESTWG_CHAIN=<name>.
.TP
.B validate
Strictly parse the chain and referenced WireGuard files without changing the
host.
.TP
.B plan
Print the executable JSON plan, including pinned endpoints, interface
addresses, allowed routes, implementation resources, and calculated MTUs.
.TP
.B up
Create a persistent chain. With
.BR --wait ,
wait until every hop has completed a WireGuard handshake or the duration
expires. A readiness timeout leaves the configured chain up for inspection.
.TP
.B down
Remove a chain in reverse order. Teardown refuses to proceed while a
NestWG-managed payload command holds a shared lifecycle lock or while protected
host routes are attached.
.TP
.B attach
Expose the innermost WireGuard device to the host and route each supplied
destination CIDR through it. The device socket remains inside the preceding
VPN layer, preserving nesting without forwarding or NAT. Requested CIDRs must
be covered by the exit peer AllowedIPs and must not replace existing exact
routes. Every live route receives an unreachable backup so loss of the device
cannot fall back to the normal default route.
.TP
.B detach
Return the exit WireGuard device to the isolated payload network and remove
active VPN routes and fail-closed unreachable routes. Ordinary host routing
resumes for those destinations.
.TP
.B status
Show active chains, live handshake and transfer information, host attachment
state, and protected routes.
.TP
.B diagnose
Check runtime construction, every hop handshake, host device state, active
CIDR routes, and their fail-closed unreachable alternatives.
.TP
.B exec
Run one command through the fail-closed VPN connection with private DNS.
When invoked via sudo, the command runs as the invoking user.
.TP
.B shell
Open the invoking user's shell through an already connected persistent VPN.
.TP
.B doctor
Check privileges, netlink access, kernel WireGuard support, resolver mounting,
and runtime-state security.
.TP
.B recover
Remove strictly named, process-free orphan namespaces after a chain state file
has been lost. Recovery refuses active namespaces and chains that still have a
state record.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/nestwg
Root-owned mode-0700 locks, recovery state, and per-chain resolver files.
.TP
.I /run/netns/nwg-<chain>-*
Named network namespaces owned by a chain.
.SH SECURITY
WireGuard configuration files must not be accessible by group or other users.
Hook commands from wg-quick files are ignored and never executed. Application
protocol encryption such as TLS remains necessary; the exit can observe
plaintext application traffic and final destinations.
.PP
NestWG is pre-release software and must not yet be relied upon as a security or
anonymity boundary. See SECURITY.md and docs/threat-model.md.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Lifecycle and inspection commands return zero on success and nonzero on
validation, readiness, privilege, or kernel-operation failure. The
.B exec
and
.BR shell ,
and
.B connect
commands preserve the payload process exit status.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wg (8),
.BR ip-netns (8),
.BR wireguard (8)
